**Read:** [Demand-controlled cleanroom filtration — Faulkner, Fisk and Walton](../sources/Demand-Controlled-Cleanroom-Filtration-Faulkner-Fisk-Walton.lingua#r_2W1ADE030W9RFA8ATDKSYVT6XP), control routines; [Multizone airflow and contaminants — Dols and Polidoro, CONTAM 3.4](../sources/CONTAM-Multizone-Airflow-and-Contaminants-NIST.lingua#r_5HECDC480RSYAJ3SQ2QNFHCN46), control elements; revisit the existing dynamic measurement and control foundations.

A bounded sensor model is τs dy/dt=C−y. A transport delay can shift the input to C(t−L). For a step from C0 to C1 after that delay, y=C1+(C0−C1)exp[−(t−L)/τs]. Physical contamination can reach the process before the reported signal reaches its decision level.

For hypothetical τs=30 s, the sensor takes about 89.87 s after the input step to register 95% of its change. An additional transport delay adds to that elapsed time; increasing controller gain does not remove the delay.

**Work:** Combine a room recovery model with the sensor model and a bounded actuator response. Check the actual concentration and observed signal separately. Examine sampling interval, saturation and a failed reading. Explain which disturbances require prevention at the source or boundary rather than correction after detection.

**Return:** [Clean manufacturing and contamination control — from familiar objects to controlled processes](../Clean-manufacturing-and-contamination-control.lingua#r_7D8SQ8BVQDZQBDBGC6DP0JAMZN).
